    Run the dependency analysis from the root pom (#8225)
    
    Every module that wanted the dependency analysis had to declare the
    `maven-dependency-plugin` itself, and 71 of them
    also carried the same `ignoredUnusedDeclaredDependency` entry for
    `org.slf4j:slf4j-reload4j`. That dependency is
    declared only to provide a logger for tests, so the analysis rightfully
    reports it as unused.
    
    This pull request moves both into the root pom:
    
    - The exception for `slf4j-reload4j` is configured once, as
    `ignoredDependencies`. It cannot go into
    `ignoredUnusedDeclaredDependencies`, because a module that sets its own
    list replaces the inherited one instead of
      merging with it.
    - The plugin is activated for all modules from the root pom. 140 modules
    no longer need to declare it at all. The 89
      modules with their own analysis configuration keep their declaration.
    - Distributions (`karaf-assembly` packaging) are excluded from the
    analysis explicitly. They contain no code, and the
      analysis would otherwise report their feature dependencies as unused.
    
    Altogether, this removes about 570 lines of boilerplate from the module
    poms.
    
    ### How to test this patch
    
    Run a full build, e.g. `./mvnw clean install -DskipTests`. It should
    succeed, with the analysis reporting
    "No dependency problems found" for every module and "Skipping
    karaf-assembly project" for the distributions.
    
    To see that the analysis still runs in modules that no longer declare
    the plugin, add an unused dependency to one of
    them, e.g. `commons-io` to `modules/dictionary-regexp/pom.xml`. The
    build of that module should fail with
    "Unused declared dependencies found".
    
    ### AI Usage
    
    I used Claude Opus to find the redundant configuration, write the
    changes and run the builds and checks described
    above.
